# `[...items]`: nothing but the spread steps its iterator, and nothing closes it. One operation of the run time opens and steps, whatever is spread, and reads a list that nobody can see being iterated by index:
# the loop of the protocol stands in no function of the program. Where a list is expected nothing stands in front either: the stub of the operation asks whether it has a list as lists are made, and copies its elements.
# function all(items) { return [...items]; }
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=none+TypedOperations
# CHECK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# CHECK: [[list]]: {{*}} = NewArray
# CHECK-NOT: RequireObjectCoercible
# CHECK: AppendIterable [[list]], v0 [readsListsByIndex]
# RUN(NEVER): walk f1 mechanisms=none+TypedOperations
# NEVER-NOT: @@iterator
# NEVER-NOT: isLoopHead
# RUN(CLOSED): walk f1 mechanisms=none
# CLOSED: = PropGet {{*}}key=@@iterator
# CLOSED: isLoopHead=true

module m0 path="main.js" displayPath="main.js" body=f0
entry module=m0
function f0 module=m0 kind=Module thisType={type=anything} results=[{type=anything}] {
  b0():
    v0 = Builtin builtin=GlobalThis
    v1 = PropGet v0 key="sink" [isFreeName]
    v2 = NewClosure function=f1
    v3 = Call v1, -, v2
    Return v3
}
function f1 name="all" module=m0 length=1 thisType={type=anything} parameters=[{type=anything}] results=[{type=anything}] {
  b0(v0: anything):
    v1 = NewArray contents=anything
    AppendSpread v1, v0
    Return v1
}
